      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to analyze the individual components of the function: \\( \\tan x \\) and \\( \\cot x \\). The period of both \\( \\tan x \\) and \\( \\cot x \\) is \\( \\pi \\), meaning that \\( \\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x \\) and \\( \\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nThe function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is a sum of two periodic functions with periods \\( \\pi \\). The period of the sum of two periodic functions is the least common multiple (LCM) of their individual periods. For multiples of \\( \\pi \\), the common periods are \\( \\pi \\) and \\( 2\\pi \\). The LCM of \\( \\pi \\) and \\( 2\\pi \\) is \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nNext, let's derive the period step-by-step:\n1. \\( \\tan \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\cot x \\) and \\( \\cot \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\tan x \\).\n2. \\( \\tan \\left( x + \\pi \\right) = \\tan x \\)\n3. \\( \\cot \\left( x + \\pi \\right) = \\cot x \\)\n4. Analyze \\( x = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\):\n    \\[\n    f \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\tan \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right)\n    \\]\n    For other multiples, wrap around:\n    \\[\n    f( 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} ) = f \\left( \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\tan \\left( -\\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right)\n    \\]\n    Analyze the symmetry resulting periodicity loop for \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\).\n\nThus, the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) repeats after \\( 2\\pi \\). Therefore, the period of \\( y \\) is \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nThe correct answer is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the function y = tan(x) + cot(x)\ny = sp.tan(x) + 1/cot(x)\n\n# Find the period of the function\nperiod = sp.periodicity(y, x)\n\nprint(period)\n```",
