      "text": "$\\tan x + \\cot x = \\tan x + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$.\n$= \\frac{\\sin x + \\cos x}{\\sin x}$.\n$= \\frac{1}{\\sin x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$.\n$= 2\\cos x$.\nSince $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, we have $t = \\tan x$, and hence $y = 2t$.\nSince $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{\\sin x}$, we have $y = \\tan x + \\cot x = \\csc x + \\sec x = \\csc x + \\frac{1}{\\csc x}$.\nFor $y = \\csc x + \\sec x > 0$, $x \\in (-\\frac{\\pi}{2}; 0)$ has $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ as minimum value, $x \\in (0; \\frac{\\pi}{2})$ has $y = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ as minimum value.\nAnd since $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is the minimum value, the period is $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$.\n\nWe used the trigonometric addition formulas to find that $\\tan x + \\cot x = 2 \\cos x$. We can also find $y$ using the identity $y = \\tan x + \\cot x = \\frac{\\sin x + \\cos x}{\\sin x}$. This means we want to eliminate $\\cos x$ from the denominator, we can take inverse trig functions and use the subtraction formulas. To find the period of the function $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$, we will use the periodic property of trigonometric functions and the given problem's requirements. Here is the reasoning step-by-step:\n\n1. Let $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$.\n2. Using the identity, $y = \\frac{\\sin x + \\cos x}{\\sin x}$.\n3. Using the conditions for the period, $x \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right)$ we get a minimum value of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n4. For $x \\in (0, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$ we also get a value of $y = 1$ here. Then using the problem statement, the cycle function with the same graph looks like $y = x$. Finally attaching the function to a period $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. \n5. As well the graph $X = 1 + \\cos x \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is periodic $y = \\frac{\\tan x + \\cot x}{2}$ with a period $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nThe period of $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}$.",
